<text x="320" y="50">Address bus</text><text x="320" y="110">Data bus</text><text x="320" y="170">Control bus</text>
</g>
<g font-size="11.5" fill="#0B3B57" text-anchor="middle">
<text x="320" y="82">one-way: CPU to memory</text><text x="320" y="142">two-way: read and write</text><text x="320" y="202">two-way: control signals</text>
</g>
</svg>

The white arrows inside the CPU are the register transfers from the previous page: PC → MAR, MDR → CIR and MDR ↔ ACC. The same three buses also link the CPU to the input and output devices.
@end

@row
@box dark | Address bus
Carries the **address** of the location the CPU wants. Only the CPU sends addresses, so it is **unidirectional**: from the MAR to memory and I/O.
@end
@box alt | Data bus
Carries the **data and instructions**. Data travels into the CPU when reading and out when writing, so it is **bidirectional**.
@end
@box light | Control bus
Carries **signals** such as "memory read", "memory write" and clock pulses, and interrupt requests back to the CPU, so it is **bidirectional**.
@end
@endrow

**Bus width matters.** An address bus with n wires can carry 2<sup>n</sup> different addresses. A 32-bit address bus gives 2<sup>32</sup> = 4 294 967 296 addresses; with one byte at each address, that is 4 GiB. That is why old 32-bit computers could use at most 4 GiB of RAM.

@try
Explain why the address bus is unidirectional but the data bus is bidirectional. How many memory locations can a 24-bit address bus address?
@end



@page RISC and CISC processors
@section 3.4 Parallel Processing and Computer Systems
@plane none

Every processor understands a fixed list of machine-code instructions: its **instruction set**. A **CISC** (complex instruction set computer) offers many powerful instructions, so programs need fewer of them; a **RISC** (reduced instruction set computer) offers fewer, simpler instructions that each do one small step quickly.

@box light | Worked example: multiply two numbers stored in memory
**Task:** multiply the values at addresses 200 and 201, and store the result back at address 200.

**CISC, one instruction:** `MULT 200, 201`. The hardware loads, multiplies and stores, taking several clock cycles.

**RISC, four simple instructions:** `LOAD R1, 200` · `LOAD R2, 201` · `MUL R1, R2` · `STORE R1, 200`. Each is designed to take about one clock cycle.
@end

@box dark | Comparing the two designs
| Feature | CISC | RISC |
|---|---|---|
| Instructions | many, some very complex | fewer and simple |
| Instruction length | variable | fixed, e.g. 32 bits |
| Clock cycles per instruction | often several | usually one |
| Memory access | many instructions use memory | only LOAD and STORE |
| Registers | fewer | many general-purpose ones |
| Control unit | microprogrammed | hard-wired |
| Pipelining | harder | easier and more efficient |
| Program length | shorter: more work for the hardware | longer: more work for the compiler |
| Examples | Intel and AMD x86 chips in PCs | ARM chips in phones and Apple Macs |
@end

Neither design always wins. RISC chips are simpler and use less power, so almost every smartphone has one. Modern x86 chips blend the two, breaking complex instructions into simpler steps inside the chip.

@try
Define the core difference between RISC and CISC architectures regarding instruction size and decoding complexity.
@end



@page Pipelining: the laundry analogy
@plane none

Imagine you have three loads of laundry. Each load goes through three stages: **wash** (1 hour), **dry** (1 hour) and **fold** (1 hour). If you finish one load completely before starting the next, the washing machine stands idle while you dry and fold.

@box light | One load at a time, or a pipeline?
<svg viewBox="0 0 600 166" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="Avenir, sans-serif" font-size="11.5">
<rect width="600" height="166" rx="10" fill="#FFFFFF"/>
<g fill="#0B3B57" text-anchor="middle"><text x="112" y="15" text-anchor="end">hour</text><text x="146" y="15">1</text><text x="198" y="15">2</text><text x="250" y="15">3</text><text x="302" y="15">4</text><text x="354" y="15">5</text><text x="406" y="15">6</text><text x="458" y="15">7</text><text x="510" y="15">8</text><text x="562" y="15">9</text></g>
<text x="10" y="35" font-size="12" font-weight="800" fill="#0B3B57">One at a time</text><text x="10" y="49" fill="#0B3B57">9 hours</text>
<g font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">
<rect x="121" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#2CABBE"/><text x="146" y="40" fill="#FFFFFF">Wash 1</text>
<rect x="173" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#77E0F5"/><text x="198" y="40" fill="#0B3B57">Dry 1</text>
<rect x="225" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#0C5E86"/><text x="250" y="40" fill="#FFFFFF">Fold 1</text>
<rect x="277" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#2CABBE"/><text x="302" y="40" fill="#FFFFFF">Wash 2</text>
<rect x="329" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#77E0F5"/><text x="354" y="40" fill="#0B3B57">Dry 2</text>
<rect x="381" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#0C5E86"/><text x="406" y="40" fill="#FFFFFF">Fold 2</text>
<rect x="433" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#2CABBE"/><text x="458" y="40" fill="#FFFFFF">Wash 3</text>
<rect x="485" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#77E0F5"/><text x="510" y="40" fill="#0B3B57">Dry 3</text>
<rect x="537" y="22" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#0C5E86"/><text x="562" y="40" fill="#FFFFFF">Fold 3</text>
</g>
<text x="10" y="104" font-size="12" font-weight="800" fill="#0B3B57">Pipelined</text><text x="10" y="118" fill="#0B3B57">5 hours</text>
<g fill="#0B3B57" text-anchor="end"><text x="112" y="82">Load 1</text><text x="112" y="114">Load 2</text><text x="112" y="146">Load 3</text></g>
<g font-weight="800" text-anchor="middle">
<rect x="121" y="64" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#2CABBE"/><text x="146" y="82" fill="#FFFFFF">Wash</text>
<rect x="173" y="64" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#77E0F5"/><text x="198" y="82" fill="#0B3B57">Dry</text>
<rect x="225" y="64" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#0C5E86"/><text x="250" y="82" fill="#FFFFFF">Fold</text>
<rect x="173" y="96" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#2CABBE"/><text x="198" y="114" fill="#FFFFFF">Wash</text>
<rect x="225" y="96" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#77E0F5"/><text x="250" y="114" fill="#0B3B57">Dry</text>
<rect x="277" y="96" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#0C5E86"/><text x="302" y="114" fill="#FFFFFF">Fold</text>
<rect x="225" y="128" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#2CABBE"/><text x="250" y="146" fill="#FFFFFF">Wash</text>
<rect x="277" y="128" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#77E0F5"/><text x="302" y="146" fill="#0B3B57">Dry</text>
<rect x="329" y="128" width="50" height="28" rx="5" fill="#0C5E86"/><text x="354" y="146" fill="#FFFFFF">Fold</text>
</g>
<line x1="380" y1="58" x2="380" y2="160" stroke="#0B3B57" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="5 4"/>
<text x="392" y="104" font-size="12" font-weight="800" fill="#0B3B57">all 3 loads done</text><text x="392" y="120" fill="#0B3B57">4 hours saved</text>
</svg>
@end

**Without a pipeline:** 3 loads × 3 hours = **9 hours**. **With a pipeline:** as soon as load 1 moves to the dryer, load 2 goes into the washer. Once the pipeline is full, one load is finished **every hour**, so the total is 3 + (3 − 1) = **5 hours**. Each load still takes 3 hours from start to finish (its **latency**), but more loads are finished per hour (the **throughput** rises).

@h2 The same idea inside a CPU
Executing an instruction also happens in stages, and each stage uses a different part of the processor:
@flow Fetch :: get the instruction > Decode :: work out what it means > Execute :: the ALU does the work > Write back :: store the result

A **pipelined** processor fetches the next instruction while the current one is being decoded, just as the washer takes a new load while the dryer is busy. One load is one instruction, each machine is one stage, and each hour is one clock cycle. With k stages, n instructions take k + (n − 1) clock cycles instead of n × k.

@try
Using the laundry example, how long would 6 loads take with and without a pipeline? Explain the difference between latency and throughput.
@end



@page Pipelining and registers in RISC
@plane none

RISC processors were designed with pipelining in mind. A typical RISC pipeline has five stages, each taking **one clock cycle**: instruction fetch (**IF**), instruction decode (**ID**), operand fetch (**OF**), instruction execute (**IE**) and write back (**WB**).

@box light | Five instructions in a five-stage pipeline
<svg viewBox="0 0 600 170" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="Avenir, sans-serif" font-size="12">
<rect width="600" height="170" rx="10" fill="#FFFFFF"/>
